    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "can u give me a more intuitive way to think about that 90 degree phase shift? like if i were trying to visualize what's actually happening inside the component as the voltage and current are moving at different times?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Imagine the voltage as filling a bucket; current is the speed at which water flows in, and the capacitor's voltage only peaks once the bucket is full, meaning current must jump to its maximum just before the bucket fills up. In contrast, if the voltage were a spring being stretched, the inductor (resisting the stretch) would only move as the stretching force has already begun, causing the current to lag behind. This \"current leads voltage\" dynamic means the coil of current always flows just as the voltage is about to reach its shoulder, creating that perfect quarter-cycle offset.",
      "source": "target",
